/* ---------- topic pages: people and beings ---------- */
TP({id:'john', kind:'People', title:'John, the Writer', ch:[1,22],
 lead:`The writer calls himself John four times (1:1, 4, 9; 22:8): "your brother and partner in suffering," a prophet exiled on Patmos. He never calls himself an apostle, and he never explains which John he is, which suggests his readers knew him well.`,
 sections:[
  {h:'Who did early Christians think he was?', table:[['Writer','Date','Said'],['Justin Martyr','about 150','"John, one of the apostles of Christ" (<i>Dialogue with Trypho</i> 81.4)'],['Irenaeus','about 180','John the disciple of the Lord, seen near the end of Domitian’s reign'],['Gaius of Rome','about 200','Reportedly said it was written by the heretic Cerinthus'],['Dionysius of Alexandria','about 250','A holy man named John, but not the apostle; the Greek is too different'],['Eusebius','about 325','Perhaps John the Elder; listed the book both among the accepted books, "if it seems right," and among the spurious ones (<i>Church History</i> 3.25.2–4)']]},
  ['Which John?',`<b>John the apostle</b>, son of Zebedee, was the view of Justin and Irenaeus and of most of the church through history. <b>John the Elder</b>, a different leader in Asia mentioned by Papias (quoted in Eusebius, <i>Church History</i> 3.39.4), was proposed by Dionysius. <b>Another prophet named John</b>, otherwise unknown, is the view of many modern scholars. All agree he was a Jewish Christian prophet with deep knowledge of the Old Testament and close ties to the churches of Asia.`],
  ['His Greek',`Dionysius noticed that Revelation's Greek is rough and very different from the Gospel of John, though the two share key words like "Lamb," "Word," "witness" and "living water." Revelation's grammar sometimes breaks the normal rules, for example "from the one who is" in 1:4, where the Greek keeps God's name in the subject form even after "from." Many scholars think this is deliberate, showing respect for the divine name (compare Exodus 3:14), or the Greek of a man who thought in Hebrew or Aramaic.`],
  ['A disputed book',`Because of its authorship and its use by groups predicting the end, Revelation was slow to be accepted in the Eastern churches. Some Greek lists of Scripture leave it out, and it is still not read in the Orthodox Sunday lectionary. In the West it was accepted early. At the Reformation, Luther wrote in 1522 that his "spirit cannot accommodate itself to this book," though he softened this later; Zwingli doubted it, and Calvin wrote commentaries on every New Testament book except Revelation (and 2 and 3 John).`],
  ['A prophet',`The word "prophecy" occurs seven times in the book, and five of them name the book itself (1:3; 22:7, 10, 18, 19). John is told, "You must prophesy again" (10:11). He stands in line with Ezekiel and Daniel, receiving visions, eating a scroll, and being told what to write.`]],
 links:[['John of Patmos','John of Patmos'],['John the Apostle','John the Apostle'],['John the Presbyter','John the Presbyter'],['Dionysius of Alexandria','Dionysius of Alexandria'],['Development of the New Testament canon','Development of the New Testament canon']],
 sources:['koester-rev','aune-rev','irenaeus']});

TP({id:'son-of-man', kind:'People', title:'"One Like a Son of Man"', ch:[1,14],
 lead:`John's first vision of Jesus (1:13–16) and the harvester on the cloud (14:14) both use Daniel's phrase "one like a son of man" (Daniel 7:13). Every detail of the first vision comes from the Old Testament, and most of them reappear in the letters to the churches.`,
 sections:[
  {h:'The portrait and where it comes from', table:[['Feature','Old Testament source','Used again in'],['Long robe and gold sash (1:13)','The high priest (Exodus 28:4); the angel of Daniel 10:5','—'],['Hair white as wool and snow (1:14)','The Ancient of Days (Daniel 7:9)','—'],['Eyes like flames of fire (1:14)','Daniel 10:6','Thyatira (2:18); the rider (19:12)'],['Feet like polished bronze (1:15)','Daniel 10:6; Ezekiel 1:7','Thyatira (2:18)'],['Voice like rushing waters (1:15)','God’s voice (Ezekiel 43:2)','—'],['Seven stars in his right hand (1:16)','—','Ephesus (2:1); Sardis (3:1)'],['Sharp two-edged sword from his mouth (1:16)','Isaiah 11:4; Isaiah 49:2','Pergamum (2:12, 16); the rider (19:15)'],['Face like the sun (1:16)','Judges 5:31; the Transfiguration (Matthew 17:2)','—'],['First and Last, died and alive (1:17–18)','Isaiah 44:6','Smyrna (2:8)'],['Keys of death and Hades (1:18)','Isaiah 22:22','Philadelphia, key of David (3:7)']]},
  ['God’s features on a human figure',`In Daniel 7, the "one like a son of man" comes to the Ancient of Days, whose hair is white like wool. In Revelation 1, the son of man himself has the white hair. John fuses the two figures: Jesus shares God's identity. This is one of the earliest and boldest statements of Jesus's divinity in the New Testament, made by picture rather than by argument.`],
  ['Jesus’s favorite title',`"Son of Man" is the title Jesus used for himself most often in the Gospels, about eighty times. Before the high priest he said, "And you will see the Son of Man sitting at the Power's right hand and coming with the clouds of the sky" (Mark 14:62). In Jewish writings of the time, especially the part of 1 Enoch called the Similitudes (chapters 37–71), the Son of Man was a heavenly judge hidden before creation. 4 Ezra 13 pictures "something like a man" rising from the sea and flying with the clouds.`],
  ['The harvester',`In 14:14 "one like a son of man" sits on a white cloud with a gold crown and a sharp sickle, reaping the earth. Most readers take him as Christ gathering his people; a few, noting that an angel gives him orders (14:15), think he is an angel.`]],
 views:{P:`The risen Christ present with his churches and coming in judgment on his enemies soon.`,H:`Christ ruling his church through all history.`,F:`Christ in glory, as he will appear at his return.`,I:`Christ as he really is, always present among his churches.`},
 links:[['Son of man','Son of man (Christianity)'],['Ancient of Days','Ancient of Days'],['Book of Enoch','Book of Enoch']],
 sources:['bauckham-theology','beale-rev']});

TP({id:'lamb', kind:'People', title:'The Lamb', ch:[5,6,7,12,13,14,17,19,21,22],
 lead:`<i>Arnion</i>, "lamb," is Revelation's main name for Jesus: 29 uses, 28 of them for Christ. The other one is the land beast, which has horns "like a lamb" (13:11). The whole book can be read as the story of how a slaughtered lamb rules the world.`,
 sections:[
  ['Lion and Lamb',`An elder announces "the Lion from the tribe of Judah, the Root of David" (5:5, from Genesis 49:9 and Isaiah 11:1, 10), and John turns and sees a Lamb who "looked as though he had been slaughtered" (5:6). John hears one thing and sees another, and the second explains the first. The book redefines power: the Messiah wins by dying.`],
  ['Which lamb?',`The Passover lamb (Exodus 12), whose blood marked the doors and freed Israel from Egypt; Revelation's plagues and song of Moses keep this in view. The lamb led to slaughter (Isaiah 53:7). "the Lamb of God, the one who takes the world's sin away" (John 1:29, though John's Gospel uses a different Greek word, <i>amnos</i>).`],
  ['A lamb with horns',`The Lamb has "seven horns and seven eyes" (5:6): all power and all sight, from Zechariah's seven lamps, which are "the LORD's eyes, and they roam over all the earth" (Zechariah 4:10). A horned, fighting lamb was not new: in 1 Enoch's "Animal Apocalypse" the Jewish people are sheep, and a ram with a great horn leads them in battle (1 Enoch 90:9–12). John keeps the battle but changes the weapon: this lamb has been slaughtered.`],
  {h:'The Lamb and the beast', table:[['The Lamb','The beast'],['"As if slaughtered" (5:6)','A head "as if slaughtered," healed (13:3)'],['Seven horns, seven eyes (5:6)','Ten horns, seven heads (13:1)'],['Receives authority from God (5:7)','Receives authority from the dragon (13:2)'],['Worshiped by every creature (5:13)','Worshiped by those not in the book of life (13:8)'],['Followers sealed with his name (14:1)','Followers marked with its name (13:17)'],['Wins by dying','Wins by killing']]},
  ['What the Lamb does',`He is worshiped (5:8–14), opens the seals (6:1), causes "the anger of the Lamb" (6:16), shepherds (7:17), stands on Zion (14:1), conquers as "Lord of lords and King of kings" (17:14), marries (19:7), lights the city (21:23), and shares God's throne (22:1).`]],
 claude:{title:'Twenty-eight is four sevens',body:`Richard Bauckham noted that "Lamb" is used for Christ exactly 28 times, 7 × 4: seven for completeness, four for the world. I counted in the Greek text and found the same: 29 uses of <i>arnion</i>, one of which (13:11) is the beast. If the count is intended, John is saying in numbers what he says in words: the Lamb's victory is complete and worldwide.`, refs:'5:6; 13:11; 22:3'},
 links:[['Lamb of God','Lamb of God'],['Lion of Judah','Lion of Judah'],['Passover sacrifice','Passover sacrifice']],
 sources:['bauckham-theology','gorman-reading','bauckham-climax']});

TP({id:'angels', kind:'People', title:'Angels in Revelation', ch:[1,7,8,10,14,18,20,22],
 lead:`<i>Angelos</i> means "messenger." Revelation mentions angels 67 times, more than any other book of the Bible. They carry messages, blow trumpets, pour bowls, hold back winds, guard gates and lead worship.`,
 sections:[
  {h:'What angels do in Revelation', table:[['Role','Examples'],['Bring the revelation','"He sent his angel to his servant John" (1:1; 22:6, 16)'],['Over the churches','"The seven stars are angels of the seven churches" (1:20)'],['Lead worship','Myriads around the throne (5:11; 7:11)'],['Control nature','Angels of the four winds (7:1), of fire (14:18), of the waters (16:5)'],['Carry out judgments','Seven trumpets (8–11), seven bowls (16)'],['Proclaim','Three angels with three messages (14:6–11)'],['Fight','Michael and his angels against the dragon (12:7)'],['Guard','Twelve angels at the city gates (21:12)']]},
  ['The angels of the churches',`"The seven stars are angels of the seven churches" (1:20). They may be guardian angels, or human messengers or leaders. Each letter scolds its angel for the church's sins, which suggests the angel and the church share one identity.`],
  ['The seven before God',`"The seven angels who stand in God's presence" (8:2) recall Raphael's words in Tobit 12:15: "I am Raphael, one of the seven angels who stand in attendance and go in before the glory of the Lord." 1 Enoch 20 names seven archangels: Uriel, Raphael, Raguel, Michael, Sariel, Gabriel and Remiel. Revelation names only Michael (12:7) and Abaddon/Apollyon, the angel of the abyss (9:11).`],
  ['The mighty angel',`The angel of chapter 10 is "dressed in a cloud, and the rainbow was over his head. His face was like the sun, and his feet were like pillars of fire" (10:1). Some readers think he is Christ himself, since these are the features of the son of man (1:15–16) and of God's throne (4:3). Most think he is a great angel reflecting Christ's glory.`],
  ['Not to be worshiped',`Twice John falls down to worship an angel and is stopped: "Don't do that! I am a servant with you and with your brothers and sisters... Worship God!" (19:10; 22:8–9). Some early Christians were tempted to worship angels (Colossians 2:18), and some Jewish writings show the same caution.`]],
 views:{P:`Angels are God's agents in the first-century judgments.`,H:`Angels sometimes stand for human figures in church history, such as reformers or missionaries.`,F:`Literal angels carrying out the end-time judgments.`,I:`Angels show that heaven is behind the events of every age.`},
 links:[['Angels in Christianity','Angels in Christianity'],['Seven Archangels','Seven Archangels'],['Book of Tobit','Book of Tobit']],
 sources:['aune-rev','koester-rev']});

TP({id:'living-creatures', kind:'People', title:'The Four Living Creatures', ch:[4,5,6,15,19],
 lead:`Around God's throne are four <i>zōa</i>, "living ones," like a lion, an ox, a human and an eagle, each with six wings and covered with eyes (4:6–8). They never stop saying, "Holy, holy, holy."`,
 sections:[
  {h:'Three visions compared', table:[['','Ezekiel 1 and 10 (cherubim)','Isaiah 6 (seraphim)','Revelation 4'],['How many','Four','Two or more','Four'],['Faces','Each has four: human, lion, ox, eagle','Not described','One each: lion, ox, human, eagle'],['Wings','Four each','Six each','Six each'],['Eyes','Wheels full of eyes','—','Covered with eyes'],['Cry','—','"Holy, holy, holy"','"Holy, holy, holy"']]},
  ['Meaning',`Many read them as all living creation worshiping God: wild animals, tame animals, humans and birds. A Jewish saying, recorded later, calls these four the mightiest of their kinds: the lion among wild beasts, the ox among tame ones, the eagle among birds, and humanity over all. In Revelation they also call out the four horsemen ("Come!" 6:1–7) and hand the bowls to the angels (15:7): creation itself takes part in judging those who destroy it (11:18).`],
  ['The four Gospels',`From the 100s on, Christian writers matched the four creatures to the four Gospels, but not always the same way. Irenaeus gave John the lion and Mark the eagle (<i>Against Heresies</i> 3.11.8); Augustine gave Matthew the lion and Mark the man. The pairing that won out in Christian art is Jerome's: Matthew the man, Mark the lion, Luke the ox and John the eagle. You can see them in medieval Bibles, church windows and the Book of Kells.`],
  ['KJV wording',`The KJV calls them "four beasts," the same word it uses for the monster of chapter 13, though the Greek words differ (<i>zōa</i> versus <i>thērion</i>). Modern translations say "living creatures."`]],
 claude:{title:'Israel’s camp around the throne',body:`In the wilderness Israel camped in four groups around the tabernacle (Numbers 2). Medieval Jewish tradition (for example Ibn Ezra, and Numbers Rabbah 2:7) gives the lead tribes banners: Judah a lion, Reuben a man, Ephraim an ox, Dan an eagle. If those banners are older than the written sources, the throne room is Israel's camp around God, and "the Lion from the tribe of Judah" (5:5) is named right beside the lion-faced creature. The banner tradition is written down later than Revelation, so this is a possible echo, not a proof.`, refs:'Numbers 2:1–34; Revelation 4:6–8; Revelation 5:5'},
 links:[['Living creatures','Living creatures (Bible)'],['Cherub','Cherub'],['Seraph','Seraph'],['Tetramorph','Tetramorph']],
 sources:['aune-rev','beale-rev']});

TP({id:'elders', kind:'People', title:'The Twenty-Four Elders', ch:[4,5,7,11,19],
 lead:`Twenty-four elders in white, with gold crowns, sit on thrones around God (4:4). They fall down, lay their crowns before the throne, hold harps and bowls of incense, and sing.`,
 sections:[
  {h:'Where they appear', table:[['Verse','What they do'],['4:4, 10–11','Sit on thrones; lay down their crowns; sing "You are worthy"'],['5:5','One tells John not to weep: the Lion has won'],['5:8–10','Hold harps and bowls of incense, "the prayers of God’s people"; sing the new song'],['7:13–14','One explains who the great crowd in white is'],['11:16–18','Worship when the seventh trumpet sounds'],['19:4','Say "Amen. Hallelujah!"']]},
  ['Who are they?',`<b>The whole people of God:</b> 12 tribes plus 12 apostles, which matches the city's 12 gates and 12 foundations (21:12–14). <b>Angels</b> who lead heavenly worship; one acts as an interpreter (7:13), the way angels do in Daniel (Daniel 7:16). <b>The raptured church</b> in heaven, crowned and seated (many dispensationalists). <b>A heavenly council</b> like the one in Isaiah 24:23, where "before his elders there will be glory."`],
  ['Twenty-four in the temple',`David divided the priests into 24 divisions to serve in turn (1 Chronicles 24:7–19), and the temple musicians into 24 groups who "prophesied with harps" (1 Chronicles 25:1–31). The elders hold harps and bowls of incense, which are exactly the tasks of musicians and priests.`],
  ['"Made them" or "made us"?',`In the KJV the elders sing that Christ "hast redeemed us to God... and hast made us unto our God kings and priests" (5:9–10, KJV). The oldest manuscripts read "them," not "us." If "us" is right, the elders are redeemed humans; if "them" is right, they may be angels singing about humans. Most modern translations follow "them."`],
  ['Laying down crowns',`Their crowns are victory wreaths (<i>stephanoi</i>). Laying them before the throne echoes the way kings submitted to Rome: the Armenian king Tiridates laid his diadem before Nero's statue (Tacitus, <i>Annals</i> 15.29). Every honor they have, they hand back to God.`]],
 views:{P:`The leaders of the people of God, Old and New Testament, worshiping in heaven.`,H:`The church in heaven through history.`,F:`The raptured church, crowned and rewarded, already in heaven before the tribulation.`,I:`The whole people of God, represented in worship around the throne.`},
 links:[['Twenty-four elders','Twenty-four elders'],['Priestly divisions','Priestly divisions']],
 sources:['beale-rev','walvoord-rev','aune-rev']});

TP({id:'michael', kind:'People', title:'Michael', ch:[12],
 lead:`The archangel who leads heaven's armies and throws the dragon out of heaven (12:7–9). His name is a question: <i>Mi-ka-el</i>, "Who is like God?"`,
 sections:[
  ['In Scripture',`Daniel calls him "one of the leading princes" and "the great prince who stands over your people," who will arise in a time of distress like no other (Daniel 10:13, 21; Daniel 12:1). Jude 9 has him disputing with the devil over Moses's body, a story from a lost Jewish book called the Assumption of Moses. Paul's "voice of the archangel" at Christ's return (1 Thessalonians 4:16) may refer to him.`],
  ['In Jewish writings',`1 Enoch lists him among seven archangels, set over Israel (1 Enoch 20:5). In the Dead Sea Scrolls' <i>War Scroll</i>, God raises up "the dominion of Michael" among the angels and the kingdom of Israel among people for the final battle against darkness (War Scroll, 1QM column 17, lines 6–7). Revelation 12 draws on this hope.`],
  ['When was the war in heaven?',`Readers disagree. (1) At the beginning, when Satan first rebelled; this is the version popularized by Milton's <i>Paradise Lost</i>. (2) At Jesus's death and exaltation: the war follows the child being "snatched up to God" (12:5), and the victory song says "now the salvation... has come" because the saints won "because of the blood of the Lamb" (12:10–11); compare Jesus's words "I was watching Satan fall like lightning" (Luke 10:18) and "Now this world's ruler will be thrown out" (John 12:31). (3) In the future, in the middle of the tribulation (many futurists).`],
  ['Michael and Jesus',`Jehovah's Witnesses identify Michael with the pre-human Christ, whom they see as a created being. Seventh-day Adventists also use "Michael" as a name for Christ, but affirm that he is fully God and not an angel. Most Christians see Michael as an angel acting because of the Lamb's victory.`],
  ['In later tradition',`Michael became the most honored angel in Christian devotion: Mont-Saint-Michel in France and Saint Michael's Mount in England, countless churches, and the Catholic "Prayer to Saint Michael" composed under Pope Leo XIII in 1886.`]],
 links:[['Michael (archangel)','Michael (archangel)'],['War in Heaven','War in Heaven'],['War Scroll','War Scroll']],
 sources:['koester-rev','aune-rev']});

TP({id:'dragon', kind:'People', title:'The Dragon', ch:[12,13,16,20],
 lead:`"The ancient serpent who is called the devil and Satan, the deceiver of the whole world" (12:9). This is one of the few symbols Revelation decodes outright, and the dragon is the enemy behind every other enemy in the book.`,
 sections:[
  {h:'His names', table:[['Name','Meaning','Verses'],['The great dragon','<i>Drakōn</i>, a huge serpent','12:3, 9; 20:2'],['The ancient serpent','The snake of Eden','12:9; 20:2'],['The devil','<i>Diabolos</i>, "slanderer"','12:9, 12; 20:2, 10'],['Satan','Hebrew "adversary, accuser"','2:9, 13, 24; 3:9; 12:9; 20:2, 7'],['The accuser','Prosecutor in God’s court (Job 1–2; Zechariah 3:1)','12:10'],['The deceiver','"Who leads the whole world astray"','12:9; 20:3, 8, 10']]},
  ['His features',`Red, with seven heads and seven royal crowns and ten horns (12:3). The crowns sit on his heads, not his horns (the sea beast has them on its horns, 13:1): he claims to be king. Red may stand for murder: Jesus said of the devil, "From the start he was a killer of people" (John 8:44). His tail sweeps a third of the stars to the earth (12:4), like the arrogant horn in Daniel 8:10.`],
  ['Background',`The serpent of Eden (Genesis 3), and the promise that the woman's offspring would crush its head (Genesis 3:15). Leviathan, "the crooked serpent" and "the dragon that lives in the sea" (Isaiah 27:1; compare Psalm 74:13–14). Pharaoh as "great sea monster lying among your Nile channels" (Ezekiel 29:3). The ancient combat myths of a sea monster defeated by a god (see "End-times stories from other traditions").`],
  ['His career in Revelation',`He waits to devour the child (12:4), loses the war in heaven (12:7–9), is thrown down to earth "full of great rage... knowing he has only a little time" (12:12), pursues the woman (12:13–17), gives his power to the beast (13:2), sends out frog-like spirits to gather the kings (16:13), is bound for a thousand years (20:2–3), is released, deceives the nations again, and is thrown into the lake of fire forever (20:7–10).`]],
 views:{P:`Satan working through Rome and through Jerusalem's leaders against the early church.`,H:`Satan working through pagan Rome and later through other powers in history.`,F:`Satan's final, intensified activity during the tribulation, then his literal binding.`,I:`The spiritual power behind every persecuting system, already defeated by the cross.`},
 links:[['Satan','Satan'],['Leviathan','Leviathan'],['Chaoskampf','Chaoskampf'],['The Great Red Dragon paintings','The Great Red Dragon paintings']],
 sources:['collins-combat','koester-rev']});

TP({id:'beasts', kind:'People', title:'The Beasts', ch:[11,13,17,19],
 lead:`<i>Thērion</i>, "wild beast," appears 39 times. Revelation has a beast from the abyss (11:7), a beast from the sea (13:1), a beast from the earth (13:11, later called the false prophet), and a scarlet beast (17:3) that seems to be the sea beast in its final form. The study page "The beasts" lays out every reading; this page summarizes.`,
 sections:[
  {h:'The two beasts of chapter 13', table:[['','Beast from the sea (13:1–10)','Beast from the earth (13:11–18)'],['Looks like','Leopard, bear, lion; ten horns, seven heads','Two horns like a lamb; speaks like a dragon'],['Power from','The dragon','Acts on the first beast’s authority'],['Does','Blasphemes, makes war on the saints, rules every nation','Performs signs, makes an image, enforces the mark'],['Later called','The beast (17; 19)','The false prophet (16:13; 19:20)'],['First-century match','Rome and its emperors','The priests and promoters of emperor worship'],['End','Thrown alive into the lake of fire (19:20)','Thrown alive into the lake of fire (19:20)']]},
  ['The sea beast',`Leopard body, bear's feet, lion's mouth, ten crowned horns, seven heads (13:1–2): all four of Daniel's beasts rolled into one (Daniel 7:3–8). Daniel says his beasts are kingdoms (Daniel 7:17, 23). Its first referent is almost certainly Rome; futurists add a final world empire and ruler.`],
  ['"Names that insulted God"',`The beast's heads carry "blasphemous names" (13:1). Roman emperors took titles like <i>divi filius</i> ("son of a god"), <i>Sebastos</i> ("one to be worshiped," the Greek for Augustus), "Savior" and "Lord." Domitian liked to be addressed as "our lord and god" (Suetonius, <i>Domitian</i> 13). Coins and inscriptions in Asia carried these titles everywhere.`],
  ['Its healed wound',`One head was "slaughtered" but healed (13:3), the same Greek as the Lamb "slaughtered" (5:6). The beast copies the death and resurrection of Christ. Many link this to the Nero legend (see Nero); others to the empire's near-collapse in the civil wars of AD 68–69, after which it revived under Vespasian. The Roman poet Juvenal called Domitian "a bald Nero" (<i>Satires</i> 4.38).`],
  ['The scarlet beast',`It "was, and is not, and is about to come up out of the abyss" (17:8), a parody of God "who is, who was, and who is coming" (1:4). Its seven heads are seven mountains and seven kings (17:9–10), and its ten horns are ten kings who will turn on the prostitute (17:16).`]],
 views:{P:`Rome, or Nero in particular; the land beast as the imperial priesthood or Jerusalem's leaders.`,H:`Pagan Rome and then papal Rome; the land beast as the papacy's clergy.`,F:`A future world ruler (the Antichrist) and his religious ally.`,I:`Political power that claims divine honor, and the religion and propaganda that serve it, in every age.`},
 common:[`The beast is a single future dictator.`,`The beast was Rome, and stands for every empire like it.`],
 uncommon:[`In Daniel, beasts are kingdoms, not individuals; the beast is purely a system that takes many forms.`],
 links:[['The Beast (Revelation)','The Beast (Revelation)'],['Daniel 7','Daniel 7'],['Nero Redivivus legend','Nero Redivivus legend'],['Imperial cult of ancient Rome','Imperial cult of ancient Rome']],
 sources:['bauckham-theology','koester-rev','beale-rev']});

TP({id:'false-prophet', kind:'People', title:'The False Prophet', ch:[13,16,19,20],
 lead:`The beast from the earth, with two horns like a lamb and a dragon's voice (13:11). From 16:13 on it is called "the false prophet." It is the propaganda minister of the beast: religious, persuasive and deadly.`,
 sections:[
  {h:'What it does', table:[['Action','Verse','A copy of'],['Makes everyone worship the first beast','13:12','The Spirit leading people to worship Christ'],['Calls fire down from heaven','13:13','Elijah on Mount Carmel (1 Kings 18:38); the two witnesses (11:5)'],['Gives breath to the image, which speaks','13:15','God breathing life into Adam (Genesis 2:7); the witnesses receiving "the breath of life" (11:11)'],['Kills those who will not worship','13:15','Nebuchadnezzar’s gold statue (Daniel 3)'],['Puts a mark on hand or forehead','13:16','God’s seal (7:3)'],['Sends out frog-like spirits','16:13–14','The Egyptian frogs (Exodus 8)']]},
  ['Background',`Deuteronomy 13:1–3: a prophet who gives a sign that comes true but leads people to other gods is still false. Jesus warned, "false messiahs and false prophets will rise up. They will give great signs and wonders, so that even the chosen would be led astray, if that were possible" (Matthew 24:24). Paul speaks of the lawless one coming "with every kind of power, and with signs and wonders of falsehood" (2 Thessalonians 2:9).`],
  ['First-century setting',`In Asia, the provincial council and local elites built emperor temples, paid for festivals and served as high priests of the cult. They made worship of the emperor a civic duty. Talking statues and faked wonders were well known. Lucian describes Alexander of Abonoteichus, a "false prophet" of the 100s AD, who made a tame snake with a puppet head called Glycon that gave oracles through a hidden tube (<i>Alexander the False Prophet</i>). Christian stories said Simon Magus made statues walk.`],
  ['Lamb’s horns, dragon’s voice',`It looks like the Lamb and sounds like the dragon. Jesus warned of false prophets: "They come to you dressed up as sheep, but underneath they are wolves out for prey" (Matthew 7:15). The danger is not obvious evil but religion that serves power.`]],
 views:{P:`The priests of the imperial cult in Asia, or the Jewish leaders who cooperated with Rome.`,H:`The papacy or the Roman clergy who enforced the church's power.`,F:`A future religious leader who promotes the Antichrist and enforces his mark.`,I:`Any religion, media or ideology that persuades people to worship power.`},
 links:[['False prophet','False prophet'],['Glycon','Glycon'],['Alexander of Abonoteichus','Alexander of Abonoteichus']],
 sources:['koester-rev','friesen-cults']});

TP({id:'unholy-trinity', kind:'Ideas', title:'The Unholy Trinity', ch:[12,13,16,19,20],
 lead:`The dragon, the beast and the false prophet act together (16:13), and each copies a person of God. Evil in Revelation is never original. It can only imitate.`,
 sections:[
  {h:'The copies', table:[['God','The counterfeit'],['The Father gives the Son his throne and authority (2:27; 3:21)','The dragon gives the beast "his power, his throne, and great authority" (13:2)'],['The Lamb "as if slaughtered," alive (5:6)','The beast’s head "as if slaughtered," healed (13:3)'],['God "who is, who was, and who is coming" (1:4)','The beast "was, and is not, and is about to come up" (17:8)'],['The Spirit leads people to worship Christ (19:10)','The false prophet leads people to worship the beast (13:12)'],['God gives breath of life (11:11)','The false prophet gives breath to the image (13:15)'],['God’s seal on foreheads (7:3)','The beast’s mark on foreheads (13:16)'],['The bride, the holy city (21:2)','The prostitute, the great city (17:1–5)'],['Worship of the Lamb by every nation (5:9)','Worship of the beast by the whole earth (13:3–4)']]},
  ['Why it matters',`Revelation teaches readers to spot the fake by knowing the real thing well. The beast looks like the Lamb in some ways, and the false prophet looks like a lamb outright. The test is whom the worship goes to and what kind of power is used: the Lamb wins by dying, the beast by killing.`],
  ['Their end',`They are judged in reverse order of their appearance: Babylon falls (18), the beast and false prophet are thrown into the lake of fire (19:20), and finally the dragon joins them (20:10).`]],
 views:{P:`Satan, Rome (or Nero) and the imperial priesthood.`,H:`Satan, the papacy and its supporting powers.`,F:`Satan, the Antichrist and the false prophet of the end times.`,I:`The spiritual, political and religious forms of evil in every age.`},
 links:[['Unholy trinity','Unholy trinity']],
 sources:['bauckham-theology','koester-rev']});

TP({id:'two-witnesses', kind:'People', title:'The Two Witnesses', ch:[11],
 lead:`Two prophets in sackcloth who prophesy for 1,260 days, are killed by the beast, lie dead for 3½ days while the world celebrates, and rise and go up to heaven in a cloud (11:3–12).`,
 sections:[
  {h:'Who are they?', table:[['Identification','Held by','Reasoning'],['Moses and Elijah','Many futurists','They turn water to blood and shut the sky (Exodus 7:17–20; 1 Kings 17:1); both appeared with Jesus at the Transfiguration (Mark 9:4)'],['Enoch and Elijah','Hippolytus, Tertullian and much of the early church','The two who never died (Genesis 5:24; 2 Kings 2:11)'],['The witnessing church','Many idealists and amillennialists','They are "two lampstands," and lampstands are churches (1:20); two witnesses make testimony valid (Deuteronomy 19:15)'],['Peter and Paul','Some preterists','Both killed in Rome under Nero'],['The Law and the Prophets, or the two Testaments','Some historicists','God’s word suppressed and revived'],['Two future individuals','Many futurists','Real prophets in a rebuilt Jerusalem']]},
  ['Zechariah 4',`"These are the two olive trees and the two lampstands standing in the presence of the Lord of the earth" (11:4) quotes Zechariah 4:3, 11–14, where two olive trees feed a lampstand. In Zechariah they are Zerubbabel the governor and Joshua the high priest, a king and a priest. Revelation's witnesses are "a kingdom and priests" (1:6) in action.`],
  ['Their story is Jesus’s story',`They witness, are killed in "the great city... where their Lord too was crucified" (11:8), lie dead in public for three days, are raised when "the breath of life from God" enters them (11:11; compare Ezekiel 37:10), and go up in a cloud while their enemies watch (compare Acts 1:9). Then "the rest were filled with fear and gave glory to the God of heaven" (11:13): their death does what the plagues could not.`]],
 claude:{title:'Israel and the church as the two branches',body:`Zechariah 4 has two olive trees feeding one lampstand; Romans 11 has one olive tree with natural branches (Israel) and grafted branches (the nations). Revelation 11 has two olive trees and two lampstands. My speculation: the two lampstands are believing Israel and the church from the nations, witnessing side by side as "a kingdom and priests" (1:6). That would explain why there are two, why they share one story of death and resurrection, and why they appear in Jerusalem. I have not found this in the major commentaries.`, refs:'Revelation 11:3–4; Zechariah 4:11–14; Romans 11:17–24; Revelation 1:6; Revelation 1:20'},
 views:{P:`Christian witnesses in Jerusalem before AD 70, or Peter and Paul.`,H:`The faithful witnesses of the Reformation era, or the two Testaments.`,F:`Two literal prophets in Jerusalem during the tribulation.`,I:`The witnessing church in every age, suffering and vindicated.`},
 links:[['Two witnesses','Two witnesses'],['Transfiguration of Jesus','Transfiguration of Jesus'],['Book of Zechariah','Book of Zechariah']],
 sources:['bauckham-theology','gregg-four-views','beale-rev']});

TP({id:'woman-of-ch12', kind:'People', title:'The Woman Clothed with the Sun', ch:[12],
 lead:`"A great sign appeared in heaven: a woman dressed in the sun, with the moon under her feet and a crown of twelve stars on her head" (12:1). She gives birth to the Messiah, escapes the dragon, and is protected in the wilderness.`,
 sections:[
  ['Israel',`Joseph dreamed that the sun, moon and eleven stars bowed to him, meaning Jacob, Rachel and his brothers (Genesis 37:9): sun, moon and twelve stars are the family of Israel. The prophets picture Zion as a woman in labor: "Before she was in labor, she gave birth" (Isaiah 66:7–8); "Twist in pain and push, Daughter Zion, like a woman in labor" (Micah 4:10).`],
  ['Mary',`Catholic and Orthodox tradition see Mary, the mother of Jesus, here, and the image shapes much Christian art: Mary standing on the moon crowned with stars. Our Lady of Guadalupe (Mexico, 1531) is pictured this way. The Catholic Church reads 11:19–12:10 on the Feast of the Assumption (August 15). Many Protestant scholars accept a secondary reference to Mary within the larger picture of Israel.`],
  ['The church',`After the birth she is persecuted and protected (12:13–17), carried on eagle's wings into the wilderness as Israel was (Exodus 19:4), and fed there 1,260 days. "The rest of her offspring" are those who "hold on to the testimony of Jesus" (12:17). The woman continues as the people of God after Christ's ascension.`],
  ['Eve',`The "ancient serpent" fights the woman and her offspring, echoing God's promise in Genesis 3:15: "I will put hostility between you and the woman, and between your offspring and her offspring." Revelation 12 is that war.`],
  {myth:{culture:'Greece and Egypt', title:'Leto, Isis and the hunted mother', body:`In the Greek myth, the dragon Python pursued the pregnant goddess Leto; she escaped to an island and gave birth to Apollo, who later killed the dragon. Ephesus claimed the birth happened nearby. In Egypt, Isis hid in the marshes from Set to protect her son Horus. Revelation 12 tells the same kind of story about the true Son, perhaps on purpose. See "End-times stories from other traditions."`, link:['Leto','Leto'], link2:'probably known to John’s readers'}},
  {fact:`In 2017 a widely shared claim said a rare star alignment in the constellation Virgo on September 23 fulfilled 12:1–2 and signaled the end. Astronomers noted that similar alignments have happened before, and nothing unusual followed. John calls the woman "a sign" (<i>sēmeion</i>), a symbol, not a horoscope.`}],
 views:{P:`Faithful Israel giving birth to the Messiah, and the Jerusalem church fleeing to Pella before AD 70.`,H:`The true church through history, hidden in the wilderness during the 1,260 years of papal power.`,F:`Israel, persecuted and protected during the second half of the tribulation.`,I:`The people of God in every age, bringing forth Christ and protected by God.`},
 links:[['Woman of the Apocalypse','Woman of the Apocalypse'],['Our Lady of Guadalupe','Our Lady of Guadalupe']],
 sources:['koester-rev','collins-combat']});

TP({id:'bride', kind:'People', title:'The Bride of the Lamb', ch:[19,21,22],
 lead:`"The wedding of the Lamb has come... and his wife has made herself ready" (19:7). Later the angel shows John "the bride, the wife of the Lamb," and it is a city (21:9–10). The book's final invitation comes from her: "The Spirit and the bride say, ‘Come!’" (22:17).`,
 sections:[
  ['Background',`God is Israel's husband: "Your husband is your Maker; the LORD of Armies is his name" (Isaiah 54:5); "I will betroth you to myself forever" (Hosea 2:19–20). Paul calls Christ and the church husband and wife (Ephesians 5:25–32) and says he promised the Corinthians in marriage to one husband, Christ (2 Corinthians 11:2). Jesus told wedding parables (Matthew 22:1–14; Matthew 25:1–13) and called himself the bridegroom (Mark 2:19–20).`],
  ['A Jewish wedding',`A Jewish marriage had two stages: betrothal, which was legally binding, and, months later, the wedding, when the groom came with his friends to bring the bride to his home, followed by a feast that could last a week. The church is now "betrothed"; the wedding is the return of Christ. "Blessed are those who have been called to the wedding supper of the Lamb" (19:9).`],
  ['Her dress',`"She was allowed to dress herself... in fine linen, shining and clean... For the fine linen is the right deeds of the saints" (19:8). The dress is both a gift ("she was allowed") and her own deeds. The robes of the martyrs were "washed... white in the blood of the Lamb" (7:14).`],
  {h:'Two women, two cities', table:[['The prostitute (17–18)','The bride (19; 21)'],['Babylon, "the great city"','The new Jerusalem, "the holy city"'],['Purple, scarlet, gold and pearls (17:4)','Fine linen, bright and clean (19:8)'],['Drunk on the blood of God’s people (17:6)','Washed in the blood of the Lamb (7:14)'],['Rides the beast (17:3)','Married to the Lamb (19:7)'],['Shown "in a wilderness" (17:3)','Shown from "a huge, high mountain" (21:10)'],['Her name: "a mystery" (17:5)','Her names: the twelve tribes and apostles (21:12–14)'],['Burned and ruined (18:8)','Lasts forever (22:5)']],
   note:'Both visions open with the same words: "One of the seven angels who had the seven bowls came and said to me, ‘Come, I will show you...’" (17:1; 21:9).'}],
 views:{P:`The church, the new covenant people, replacing unfaithful Jerusalem.`,H:`The true church, finally united with Christ.`,F:`The church raptured before the tribulation, married to Christ in heaven before returning with him.`,I:`The whole people of God, perfected and united with Christ.`},
 links:[['Bride of Christ','Bride of Christ'],['Marriage of the Lamb','Marriage of the Lamb'],['Jewish wedding','Jewish wedding']],
 sources:['bauckham-theology','koester-rev']});

TP({id:'nero', kind:'People', title:'Nero', ch:[13,17],
 lead:`Roman emperor from AD 54 to 68. He blamed Christians for the great fire of Rome in 64 and had many killed; tradition says Peter and Paul died under him. For many readers he is the best candidate for the number 666 and the model for the beast.`,
 facts:[['Born','AD 37; emperor at 16'],['Infamous for','Killing his mother Agrippina (59), the persecution of 64, and stage performances'],['Death','Suicide, June 9, 68, saying "What an artist dies in me!"'],['After death','At least three men claimed to be Nero returned (69, about 80, 88)']],
 sections:[
  ['The persecution of 64',`Tacitus, no friend of Christians, describes it: "Mockery was added to their deaths. Covered with the skins of animals, they were torn by dogs and died, or were nailed to crosses, or were set on fire and burned to light the night" (<i>Annals</i> 15.44). It was the first time Rome treated Christians as a separate group to be punished.`],
  {h:'666 and 616', table:[['Spelling','Hebrew letters','Total'],['"Nero Caesar" (Greek form <i>Nerōn Kaisar</i>)','נרון קסר: N 50 + R 200 + W 6 + N 50, Q 100 + S 60 + R 200','666'],['"Nero Caesar" (Latin form <i>Nero</i>)','נרו קסר: drop the final N (50)','616']],
   note:'Some ancient manuscripts of 13:18 read 616, which is exactly the difference between the two spellings. The spelling with the final N appears in a Hebrew document from Wadi Murabba’at near the Dead Sea, dated to Nero’s reign. Many scholars find this the strongest identification; others note that hundreds of names can be made to add up to 666.'},
  ['The return of Nero',`After his suicide, rumors said he was not really dead and would return from the east with Parthian armies. At least three impostors claimed to be him (Tacitus, <i>Histories</i> 2.8; Suetonius, <i>Nero</i> 57). Jewish and Christian writings picked up the legend: the Sibylline Oracles describe a matricide king fleeing beyond the Euphrates and coming back (4.119–139; 5.361–370). The beast whose fatal wound was healed (13:3) and that "was, and is not, and is about to come up" (17:8) fits that legend.`],
  ['For the early date',`If Nero is the sixth king, "one is" (17:10), the book was written before 68. Most scholars instead think John wrote under Domitian and used Nero as the pattern: a returning Nero, a new persecutor. The poet Juvenal called Domitian "a bald Nero."`]],
 views:{P:`Nero is the beast; the book was written during or just after his reign.`,H:`Nero was the first of a long line of persecuting powers.`,F:`Nero was a type, a preview, of the future Antichrist.`,I:`Nero is a classic example of the beast's character, not its only meaning.`},
 links:[['Nero','Nero'],['Nero Redivivus legend','Nero Redivivus legend'],['Great Fire of Rome','Great Fire of Rome']],
 sources:['koester-rev','gentry-jerusalem','bauckham-theology']});

TP({id:'jezebel', kind:'People', title:'"Jezebel" of Thyatira', ch:[2],
 lead:`A woman in the Thyatira church "who calls herself a prophet" and teaches eating food sacrificed to idols and sexual sin (2:20). The chapter 2 page has a full study of her and of the prostitution language.`,
 sections:[
  ['The nickname',`The original Jezebel was the Phoenician queen of King Ahab who promoted Baal worship in Israel and persecuted the prophets (1 Kings 16:31; 1 Kings 18:4; 2 Kings 9:22). John gives the name to a real local teacher, the way we might call someone "a Judas."`],
  ['The issue',`Thyatira had many trade guilds (wool, dyers, bronze workers), whose banquets honored patron gods. Her teaching seems to have made room for Christians to join in.`]],
 links:[['Jezebel','Jezebel'],['Thyatira','Thyatira']],
 sources:['hemer-letters','ramsay-letters']});

TP({id:'balaam', kind:'People', title:'Balaam and the Nicolaitans', ch:[2],
 lead:`Two groups in the churches: those who "cling to the teaching of Balaam" (2:14) and the Nicolaitans (2:6, 15), probably the same group or close allies. Ephesus hated their practices; Pergamum tolerated them.`,
 sections:[
  ['Balaam in the Bible',`A famous foreign prophet hired by King Balak of Moab to curse Israel. God made him bless Israel instead, after his donkey saw the angel he could not (Numbers 22–24). But afterward he advised Moab to lure Israel into idol feasts and sexual sin at Baal of Peor (Numbers 25:1–2; Numbers 31:16). In the New Testament, he is the prophet who "loved the pay for doing wrong" (2 Peter 2:15; compare Jude 11).`],
  {fact:`Balaam was a real name known outside the Bible. In 1967 archaeologists at Deir Alla in Jordan found plaster wall writing from about 800 BC that begins, "The account of Balaam son of Beor, who was a seer of the gods." It is one of the oldest references outside the Bible to a person named in the Old Testament.`},
  ['The Nicolaitans',`Irenaeus (about AD 180) says they followed Nicolaus, one of the seven deacons chosen in Jerusalem (Acts 6:5), and "lived lives of unrestrained indulgence" (<i>Against Heresies</i> 1.26.3). Clement of Alexandria defended Nicolaus himself and said his followers twisted his words (<i>Miscellanies</i> 2.20; 3.4). Their teaching seems to have been that Christians could eat at idol feasts and that what you did with your body did not matter.`],
  ['Two names, one meaning?',`"Nicolaus" in Greek means "conquers the people" (<i>nikā</i> + <i>laos</i>). Jewish tradition explained "Balaam" in Hebrew as "swallows the people" (<i>bala' 'am</i>; Talmud, Sanhedrin 105a). Many scholars think John saw the two names as the same idea in two languages.`],
  ['"Conquerors of the laity"',`A popular later reading, found for example in Watchman Nee, took "Nicolaitans" as "conquering the laity" and saw in them the rise of a clergy class ruling over ordinary believers. It is a creative sermon point, not what the Nicolaitans were in the first century.`]],
 views:{P:`A real first-century group that permitted idol feasts and loose living.`,H:`The rise of clerical hierarchy and worldly compromise in the church.`,F:`A real group, and a warning against compromise in every period of church history.`,I:`Any teaching that makes peace with the culture's idols in the name of freedom.`},
 links:[['Balaam','Balaam'],['Nicolaism','Nicolaism'],['Deir Alla Inscription','Deir Alla Inscription']],
 sources:['hemer-letters','aune-rev','irenaeus-heresies-1']});

TP({id:'synagogue-of-satan', kind:'Words', title:'"Synagogue of Satan"', ch:[2,3],
 lead:`"Those who say they are Jews and are not, but are a synagogue of Satan" (2:9; 3:9), at Smyrna and Philadelphia. The topic page "Those who say they are... and are not" studies the whole pattern; this page focuses on the phrase itself.`,
 sections:[
  ['The words',`"Synagogue" means "gathering." Where the Hebrew calls Israel "the LORD's community" (Numbers 27:17; Numbers 31:16), the Greek Old Testament says <i>synagōgē</i>. John's phrase turns that around: a gathering that serves the accuser instead of the Lord. James uses "synagogue" even for a Christian assembly (James 2:2), so the word was not yet a label for "the other religion."`],
  ['In context',`John is a Jewish writer, and the dispute is about who belongs to Israel's Messiah. "Satan" means "accuser," and these local opponents may have been accusing Christians before Roman officials. Smyrna's later bishop Polycarp was martyred around AD 155 after a crowd that included local Jews called for his death (<i>Martyrdom of Polycarp</i> 12–13). John's Gospel shows the same painful split (John 8:44; John 9:22; John 16:2).`],
  ['Misuse',`The phrase has been used to justify hatred of Jews, from medieval sermons to modern hate groups. That reading turns a family argument in the first century into a weapon John never intended. Paul makes a similar point to John's in Romans 2:28–29, and in Romans 11 insists that God has not rejected his people.`]],
 links:[['Synagogue of Satan','Synagogue of Satan'],['Polycarp','Polycarp']],
 sources:['koester-rev','blount-rev']});

TP({id:'abaddon', kind:'People', title:'Abaddon / Apollyon', ch:[9],
 lead:`"They have a king over them, the angel of the abyss. In Hebrew his name is Abaddon, and in Greek he has the name Apollyon" (9:11). Abaddon is Hebrew for "destruction"; Apollyon is Greek for "Destroyer."`,
 sections:[
  ['From a place to a person',`In the Old Testament, Abaddon is a place, the realm of the dead and destruction: "Sheol and Abaddon are in full view of the LORD" (Proverbs 15:11; also Job 26:6; Job 28:22; Psalm 88:11). The Dead Sea Scrolls' Thanksgiving Hymns speak of "the torrents of Belial" bursting into Abaddon (1QH column 11). Revelation turns the place into a ruler, the king of the locusts.`],
  ['Who is he?',`Readers disagree whether he is Satan himself, a chief demon, or a good angel carrying out God's judgment (since the locusts can only harm those without God's seal, 9:4). Proverbs 30:27 says "locusts have no king," which makes it striking that these do.`],
  ['Apollo?',`Apollyon sounds very like Apollo. The locust was one of Apollo's symbols; Athens had a statue of "Apollo the Locust-God" (Pausanias 1.24.8). Emperors liked to be linked with Apollo: Augustus built him a temple, and Nero performed as Apollo the singer. Many scholars think John is making a pointed joke: Rome's patron god is really the Destroyer, king of a demonic plague.`],
  ['In literature',`John Bunyan's <i>The Pilgrim's Progress</i> (1678) has Christian fight Apollyon, a scaled monster with wings like a dragon, in the Valley of Humiliation.`]],
 views:{P:`Demonic forces behind the destruction of Jerusalem or Rome's armies.`,H:`Muhammad or the Muslim caliphs (the fifth trumpet as the Arab conquests); some Protestants said the pope.`,F:`A powerful demon released from the abyss during the tribulation.`,I:`The destructive power of evil, which turns on its own followers.`},
 links:[['Abaddon','Abaddon'],['Apollo','Apollo']],
 sources:['aune-rev','koester-rev']});

TP({id:'gog-magog', kind:'People', title:'Gog and Magog', ch:[20],
 lead:`The nations Satan gathers for the final battle after the thousand years, their "number is like the sand of the sea" (20:8). They surround "the beloved city" and are consumed by fire from heaven.`,
 sections:[
  ['Background',`Magog is a grandson of Noah through Japheth (Genesis 10:2); Josephus identified his descendants with the Scythians of the far north (<i>Antiquities</i> 1.123). In Ezekiel 38–39, Gog is a ruler "of the land of Magog," leading northern nations against restored Israel, defeated by God with earthquake, fire and hail. Revelation makes both names into peoples "at the four corners of the earth": no longer one northern enemy but all the rebellious nations.`],
  {h:'Ezekiel and Revelation compared', table:[['Ezekiel 38–39','Revelation 19–20'],['Gog of the land of Magog, prince of Meshech and Tubal','Gog and Magog, the nations at the four corners of the earth'],['Comes against Israel, restored and living in safety','Comes against the camp of God’s people after the thousand years'],['Destroyed by fire and sulfur (38:22)','Fire comes down from heaven (20:9)'],['Birds and beasts invited to eat the flesh of the mighty (39:17–20)','The "great supper of God": birds eat the flesh of kings (19:17–18)']],
   note:'Revelation splits Ezekiel’s Gog story in two: the bird feast in chapter 19 and the gathering of nations in chapter 20.'},
  ['Identifications',`Over the centuries Gog and Magog have been identified with the Goths, Huns, Mongols, Turks and others. In the 20th century, the Scofield Reference Bible (1909) and later Hal Lindsey's <i>The Late Great Planet Earth</i> (1970) taught that "Rosh, Meshech and Tubal" (Ezekiel 38:2) meant Russia, Moscow and Tobolsk. Most scholars of Hebrew read <i>rosh</i> as "chief" ("chief prince of Meshech and Tubal") and locate Meshech and Tubal in ancient Anatolia.`],
  {myth:{culture:'Islam and legend', title:'The wall of Gog and Magog', body:`The Qur'an tells of Dhul-Qarnayn, often identified with Alexander the Great, who built a wall of iron and copper to shut in Gog and Magog (Ya'juj and Ma'juj) until the end, when they will pour out (Qur'an 18:94–99; 21:96). Medieval Alexander legends spread the story across Europe and Asia. In London, giant statues named Gog and Magog have guarded the Guildhall since the 1500s.`, link:['Gog and Magog','Gog and Magog'], link2:'later legends built on the same biblical names'}}],
 views:{P:`The enemies of the church throughout the period after AD 70, or Rome's final assault.`,H:`Various invading peoples, such as the Turks.`,F:`A final rebellion of nations at the end of Christ's literal thousand-year reign.`,I:`The last, worldwide uprising of evil before the end; possibly the same battle as 19:19–21 told again.`},
 links:[['Gog and Magog','Gog and Magog'],['Magog (son of Japheth)','Magog (son of Japheth)']],
 sources:['koester-rev','boyer-prophecy']});

TP({id:'144000', kind:'Symbols', title:'The 144,000', ch:[7,14],
 lead:`Sealed servants of God, 12,000 from each of Israel's tribes (7:4–8), who later stand with the Lamb on Mount Zion with his name on their foreheads (14:1–5). Few numbers in the Bible have been read in so many ways.`,
 sections:[
  {h:'Who are they?', table:[['Reading','Held by'],['Ethnic Jewish believers sealed during the tribulation','Dispensational futurists'],['The whole church, the true Israel, counted as an army','Many amillennialists and idealists'],['Jewish Christians who escaped Jerusalem before AD 70','Many preterists'],['A literal 144,000 who go to heaven to rule with Christ; the "great crowd" lives on earth','Jehovah’s Witnesses (since 1935)'],['The faithful living at Christ’s return','Seventh-day Adventists (traditionally)']]},
  ['The strange tribe list',`Judah first; Dan and Ephraim missing; both Joseph and Manasseh included; Levi included. There are about 20 different tribe lists in the Old Testament, and none matches this one. See the page on the twelve tribes.`],
  ['A military census',`Numbers 1 counts Israel's fighting men by tribe. The 144,000 "did not soil themselves with women" (14:4), like soldiers keeping themselves pure before holy war (Deuteronomy 23:9–10; 1 Samuel 21:5). They are an army that wins by following the Lamb "wherever he goes" (14:4).`],
  ['Heard and seen',`John "heard how many had been sealed" (7:4) and then "looked, and I saw a crowd so large that no one could count it" (7:9). In 5:5–6 he heard of a Lion and saw a Lamb. Many readers think the countable army and the countless crowd are the same people seen two ways: Israel's promised army, fulfilled in a multinational crowd.`]],
 claude:{title:'Dan is missing here, but first in line at the end',body:`Dan is left out of the sealing. But in Ezekiel's vision of restored Israel, Dan is the first tribe given land (Ezekiel 48:1) and has its own gate in the city (Ezekiel 48:32). Revelation 21 borrows Ezekiel's twelve-gated city and does not list which tribes. I suspect Dan's absence in chapter 7 marks judgment on idolatry (Judges 18:30; 1 Kings 12:29) for that period, not a final exclusion. The worst tribe still gets a gate.`, refs:'Revelation 7:4–8; Ezekiel 48:1, 32; Judges 18:30; 1 Kings 12:29; Revelation 21:12'},
 links:[['144,000','144,000'],['Twelve Tribes of Israel','Twelve Tribes of Israel'],['Tribe of Dan','Tribe of Dan']],
 sources:['bauckham-theology','bauckham-climax','walvoord-rev']});
